__Suppressing mitochondrial metabolism in hibernation: Protecting against oxidative stress?__

During environmental challenges heterothermic mammals and birds conserve energy by reducing metabolism. Energetic savings come primarily from adjusting thermoregulation and allowing body temperature to fall but many mammalian heterotherms also suppress mitochondrial metabolism in non-thermogenic tissues. This suppression occurs mainly through decreased succinate dehydrogenase activity, which is reversed slowly during arousal. Succinate is a hypoxia biomarker and can drive excess mitochondrial ROS production during reoxygenation by reverse electron transport. We propose that the main selective advantage of mitochondrial metabolic suppression is to control ROS production within a rapidly changing internal environment. In hibernating ground squirrels we demonstrated that circulating O2 is very low early in arousal but rises rapidly. Succinate accumulates in the liver during torpor and arousal, however the slow reactivation of succinate dehydrogenase likely prevents its rapid oxidation, minimizing ROS production and oxidative damage. Birds likely evolved heterothermy separately from mammals and their mitochondrial metabolism may also differ. During daily torpor in ruby-throated hummingbirds, whole-animal metabolism decreases profoundly, but mitochondrial metabolism does not change significantly. In general birds have inherently low rates of mitochondrial ROS production which likely contribute to their very long lifespans. As a result, suppressing mitochondrial metabolism in daily torpor was not likely a significant selective advantage for heterothermic birds. As flying mammals, bats offer an intriguing comparison group because many species express heterothermy, but they also have inherently low mitochondrial ROS production and similar lifespans to birds. However, no data exist about mitochondrial metabolism in bats during heterothermy.
